## The Gleaners: what is gleaning?
sources:
    - id: wiki
      title: The Gleaners
      authors: Wikipedia (citing Fratello 2003, Kimmelman 1999)
      year: 2026
      finding: Salon of 1857; critics saw 'the scaffolds of 1793' and 'the Three Fates of Poverty'; sold for 3,000 francs, 300,000 francs at auction in 1889; Mme Pommery's gift to the Louvre
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gleaners
    - id: orsay
      title: Des glaneuses (RF 592)
      authors: Musée d'Orsay
      year: 2024
      finding: 1857, shown at the Salon of 1857; accepted by the state in 1890 as Mme Pommery's gift; in the Musée d'Orsay since 1986
    - id: gac
      title: Gleaners
      authors: Musée d'Orsay on Google Arts & Culture
      year: 2024
      finding: Three gleaners against a distant, busy harvest in low slanting light; a man on horseback, likely a steward, stands for the landowners
      url: https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/gleaners/GgHsT2RumWxbtw
    - id: agnsw
      title: The Gleaners 1855-1856 (etching)
      authors: Art Gallery of New South Wales
      year: 2024
      finding: Gleaners came from the poorest rural labourers and were licensed into the fields after reaping
      url: https://www.artgallery.nsw.gov.au/collection/works/368.2007/
    - id: bible
      title: Leviticus 19:9–10; the Book of Ruth
      authors: The Bible
      year: -500
      finding: Leave the gleanings for the poor and the stranger; Ruth gleans in Boaz's field

    card title "The Gleaners"

Three women bend low over a field that has already been harvested, and our question is: what are they doing, and why did it frighten people?
    image art cites ["wiki","orsay"] credit "Millet, 1857. Musée d'Orsay, Paris. Public domain." x 500 y 330 width 870 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois_Millet_-_Gleaners_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois_Millet_-_Gleaners_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.bundle [0.635,0.555,0.085,0.25] regions.cart [0.295,0.26,0.13,0.1] regions.farms [0.73,0.27,0.2,0.06] regions.grasp [0.36,0.722,0.04,0.068] regions.hands [0.165,0.625,0.245,0.17] regions.harvest [0.12,0.18,0.86,0.2] regions.haystacks [0.135,0.19,0.18,0.16] regions.left_woman [0.175,0.37,0.26,0.34] regions.middle_woman [0.36,0.415,0.27,0.37] regions.reach [0.17,0.633,0.048,0.074] regions.rider [0.866,0.283,0.033,0.05] regions.right_woman [0.62,0.3,0.25,0.56] regions.stubble [0,0.8,1,0.2] regions.women [0.17,0.3,0.7,0.57] regions.workers [0.42,0.29,0.2,0.06] draw.duration 4

This is The Gleaners, shown at the Paris Salon in 1857 by Jean-François Millet, a farmer's son who lived among farm workers.
    portrait millet x 860 y 560 width 120 of "Jean-François Millet" cameo true

    erase millet

    camera on ["art:stubble"]

Look at the ground first: short, pale stubble. The wheat has been cut and carried away.
    highlight art:stubble

    camera on ["art:left_woman","art:middle_woman"]

What the women pick up are the few ears the reapers left behind, one by one. That is gleaning.
    highlight art:hands strength strong lift true

    camera on ["art:women"]

One reaches for a single stalk, one closes her hand on another, and the third, half standing, holds her small bundle.
    highlight art:reach art:grasp art:bundle lift true

    camera reset true

It is the oldest charity of the field: the Bible tells farmers to leave the gleanings for the poor and the stranger, and Ruth gleans in the fields of Boaz.
    highlight art:women strength soft

In Millet's France the right was kept for the poorest villagers, and they were let in only after the reapers had finished.
    highlight art:stubble art:women strength soft

    ask "Who is watching them? Tap him." spot rider on art
        intro: Behind them, someone is keeping an eye on the women.
        right: There he is: a man on horseback, far off on the right.
        wrong: Look far back on the right, near the farm buildings.

    camera on ["art:rider"] zoom 3

A rider on horseback, probably the estate's steward, watching the work, and making sure the gleaners keep to the rules.
    highlight art:rider strength strong lift true

    camera on ["art:harvest"]

Around him, the real harvest: haystacks as big as houses, a cart piled high, and none of it theirs.
    highlight art:haystacks art:cart art:workers lift true

    camera on ["art:women"]

Millet sets the three women against it in a low sun, bent into heavy, rounded shapes, as solid as figures carved in stone.
    focus art:women rest paper

    pause 1

    camera reset true

So the poor fill the front of the picture, like Bruegel's ploughman; but here there is no myth left over in the corner.
    highlight art:women lift true

At the Salon, that was the trouble. Paris had seen a revolution only nine years before, in 1848, and the propertied classes feared the poor.
    highlight art:women strength soft

One critic mo (@highlight art:left_woman art:middle_woman art:right_woman@)cked the women as the Three Fates of poverty; another saw behind them the scaffolds of 1793, the Revolution's Terror.

Yet there is no anger here, and no riot. The threat was simply that three of the poorest women in France were made big, calm and impossible to overlook.
    highlight art:women lift true

    pause 0.6

    quiz "In 1889, what did it fetch?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 500 buttons_at.y 560
        intro: After the Salon, Millet sold it for three thousand francs.
        right: Three hundred thousand francs: a hundred times what Millet got.
        wrong: Fourteen years after his death, he was famous.
        * 30,000 francs
        + 300,000 francs
        * 3,000,000 francs

The scandal faded: a champagne maker gave it to the nation, and today it hangs in the Musée d'Orsay.
    highlight art:women strength soft lift true

So gleaning is living on what the harvest leaves behind, and Mille (@highlight art:women art:harvest lift true@)t puts the people who do it in front of the plenty they don't own.

    pause 0.6

    quiz "A photographer wants to do for today's poor what Millet did. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: Large in front, plenty behind: the poor made big, beside a wealth that isn't theirs.
        wrong: Millet's picture works by contrast: what is in front, and what is behind?
        * A food-bank queue, tiny figures far off
        * A full supermarket aisle, no people
        * Two women sorting leftovers, close up, a bare wall behind
        + Two women sorting leftovers, large in front, a full supermarket behind

Three centuries after Bruegel's harvesters, it is the same golde (@highlight art:women art:harvest lift true@)n field; only now the people with the least stand closest to us.

